            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Birth and Early Training</strong><br>\nGiacomo Puccini was born on December 22, 1858, in Lucca, Italy, into a family with a rich musical heritage. He began his musical education in his hometown and later studied at the Milan Conservatory. His early works showed promise and aligned with the dominant Italian opera tradition.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Early Works</strong><br>\nPuccini's first operas, such as \"Le Villi\" and \"Edgar,\" were met with mixed reviews, but they exhibited his developing style. It was \"Manon Lescaut\" in 1893 that won him national acclaim, positioning him as a worthy successor to the great Italian opera composer Verdi.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Success with 'La Bohème'</strong><br>\nLa Bohème, premiered in 1896, was a turning point in Puccini's career. Its realistic portrayal of tragic love among impoverished artists in Paris captured the public's imagination. The opera remains one of the most beloved in the repertoire, celebrated for its beautiful melodies and emotional depth.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
